#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "714c93f9-6170-52a9-849b-2e9d94805c4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-513" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bb23eb-d993-5e0d-9062-6aaf8e52a393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079659} {:answer/id #uuid "fadd6a90-fadd-59e8-8038-9fffc9db0514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079660} {:answer/id #uuid "44bdb578-5695-5086-a6a9-84f81333751f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079661} {:answer/id #uuid "667d58e4-ebda-5e7a-b337-994ff1e370da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079662} {:answer/id #uuid "755e6d3f-49f2-546a-9250-2caccf63a1ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87bb23eb-d993-5e0d-9062-6aaf8e52a393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079659} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc4d111-ad35-58c8-8b0c-22c6094b7706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c52b9d6-1dde-5a14-b98c-6e050ad50c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100495" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-2$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{-8}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225791c4-85a1-5fb9-b8b0-1b40dbdce621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288811} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfb9772-703c-5029-8934-44d2218e7823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288812} {:answer/id #uuid "a397f02c-092f-53f4-858d-8da109ab5b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288813} {:answer/id #uuid "983bd647-d383-5fa7-8e41-7ad8f6bc2982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288814} {:answer/id #uuid "cef778bd-e80a-5d89-b7c3-84b1af025f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a397f02c-092f-53f4-858d-8da109ab5b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288813} :answer-field/id #uuid "17af4f3e-9c6d-53fa-a9d3-5dfe65dcda8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40d5ff9f-2d78-5585-b446-0d19f9f03908" :question/math-academy-id "q-100643" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71522e6f-7b00-5d83-830e-5e5de40bdf73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288818} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaec9cb-c599-5ba8-ace7-af27043df259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "092eebfb-3ba9-5e64-96e1-5946b9895a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288820} {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} {:answer/id #uuid "63261215-1e5f-5294-8542-0b1c08bb9fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46d703b-f925-5221-bda1-c2baf909091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288821} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1fe9e7b-b0b1-5b9c-b4be-e4a5d4296639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7047774a-5515-50b6-ae41-3214d72cda4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-101735" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033cdbf6-5063-581f-b461-3b0faf064cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288825} {:answer/id #uuid "f4352038-3641-5325-bff8-6be663fc47e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826} {:answer/id #uuid "efc349af-feca-5ce6-bde4-0514bcf35c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd99f019-41a9-58b2-a52d-6c041413a640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288828} {:answer/id #uuid "5a921231-d28d-5536-b5ed-4286d39cbb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4352038-3641-5325-bff8-6be663fc47e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826} :answer-field/id #uuid "6439cc0a-0930-5c97-8ab6-605bd4ba7720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "881ddc28-aa2d-5caa-acf7-65aca1980081" :question/math-academy-id "q-101738" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7d6af93-db58-5dd4-b826-02c8d57a4b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288832} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f7aed7-bd56-551c-8d85-296e796fdd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288833} {:answer/id #uuid "9c00069b-30cc-5963-8f54-ded2e7bedd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288834} {:answer/id #uuid "183ec2b4-cb81-5e3a-8989-c0408a8fde0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288835} {:answer/id #uuid "de5cb732-48aa-54ea-a8d4-875b4b95ab17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c00069b-30cc-5963-8f54-ded2e7bedd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288834} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd1ea14-5b0b-5a97-ae05-f2c71ec4dd20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de7d243d-3bd6-5fa9-8a5f-d044c01534a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-118618" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1543d81-b4a1-5a9a-960f-ac8a6b526b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079785} {:answer/id #uuid "0d353053-bdb6-5bc6-b332-c28b153b5094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079786} {:answer/id #uuid "2f67c9a6-8407-5265-b6c6-2e47a9434690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079787} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecadce2-78f2-5490-a041-47e9eba22b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079788} {:answer/id #uuid "684d9015-4069-5f0c-991c-ac69c6428f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "684d9015-4069-5f0c-991c-ac69c6428f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079789} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e19088-f260-5898-b656-fdf0c268adf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d988115-5b28-5cc4-948a-8ebc728e32c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118619" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe068ee-68af-5b09-b42f-d5a531978ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288839} {:answer/id #uuid "cedd8645-a6a6-5b33-8ad1-d5aceb3ee75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288840} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70fdfb-73ba-540b-be2c-b03b091657d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288841} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbeda41-874c-5363-9d23-fdfa58537673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288842} {:answer/id #uuid "48464647-401f-59d5-8410-584cccf2c1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48464647-401f-59d5-8410-584cccf2c1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288843} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6755f4-b0c3-5433-967c-5dc8a744596a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84f2c245-aa22-51b2-9700-5a173726767d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152045" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x+4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(2x+4)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375a6cdc-fe86-5d4b-ba4c-116086885fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288846} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aadcbf-ce87-5828-a6ff-13025839c032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288847} {:answer/id #uuid "9b08d82f-4118-5c78-a99c-1330faf97e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288848} {:answer/id #uuid "b420edb3-80f8-5616-8f8f-1da7b7d3717a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288849} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9b8efe-f13b-51ad-87ac-5e2155b8f9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b420edb3-80f8-5616-8f8f-1da7b7d3717a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288849} :answer-field/id #uuid "3212ca7a-0741-5c58-9767-3754939f6f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f62763c-b254-5d25-9374-20539800a69a" :question/math-academy-id "q-152077" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+2x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{6}{(1+2x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6ad261-340f-5255-8601-541f4a965140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288853} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aa94af-ccc0-55da-a76e-b1438e6b6211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288854} {:answer/id #uuid "ead9cc6a-852d-58da-b82c-d521e12e0ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288855} {:answer/id #uuid "95ab47d6-8d26-5b4f-9cde-a469ce9d4998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288856} {:answer/id #uuid "72dd7196-dbba-5529-bd6b-a1992d7a19a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead9cc6a-852d-58da-b82c-d521e12e0ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288855} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb009ad7-cd50-5611-87fb-b939b64a88d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f5ed776-3c7e-56db-a4c7-558bcb49c8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-152755" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{{\\theta }^{6}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}2{\\theta }^{-6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{2}{5}{\\theta }^{-5})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{5}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0076a4-053c-5d0e-a5de-f00509b76469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :an