\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87fe24b0-4204-531f-a768-c03d42bf2433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080049} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf17f95-e223-5c88-93a1-e4db0cf13642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080050} {:answer/id #uuid "faab9eb1-85cf-5c01-88eb-a4d6ac61fdd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080051} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4de13f-d2a6-5947-a96a-b14a6aa5f50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080052} {:answer/id #uuid "7610169f-abbf-5536-a087-106ca671d9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d4de13f-d2a6-5947-a96a-b14a6aa5f50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080052} :answer-field/id #uuid "1051d9f0-db06-5d96-b7c7-ea6084edc1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b3895ee-aa12-5868-a13a-ac9c31fb2e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-152777" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{7}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{7}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee64568-801b-5084-87cf-de1562f008f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288867} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab61766-35a3-576f-bec9-7f7a08b07f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288868} {:answer/id #uuid "338cfd3a-108b-5e39-b1b1-0a6286be498e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288869} {:answer/id #uuid "130727f1-6952-5580-8169-6740133df6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288870} {:answer/id #uuid "e51941fe-fc63-582c-a1a4-3b35f7369225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130727f1-6952-5580-8169-6740133df6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288870} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1759d6-e74b-5313-92ad-920a6610c440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcfebbdd-eff9-56c3-a6d3-b799bf7b081e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20700" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943e251e-e564-5609-b06f-e552218c9efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080625} {:answer/id #uuid "96cb9d6c-59c0-5452-8984-e8c3c88837a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080626} {:answer/id #uuid "da9d5b8d-e5a2-561c-84fd-74bf9dc6269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080627} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e0cdec-4941-5717-aca6-d56ea1aef4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080628} {:answer/id #uuid "f641c595-7f23-5e80-a783-515379a30a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{224}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f641c595-7f23-5e80-a783-515379a30a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{224}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080629} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0875af2-58a1-5838-b1f4-359a2ec41578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764c5e30-2e09-5150-97f0-e85e059f7437" :question/math-academy-id "q-20884" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=7x+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=7x+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{(7x+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{7{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{224}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db48381e-4024-5a3d-bf0a-179de7031f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db48381e-4024-5a3d-bf0a-179de7031f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288874} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc409eb-88a2-5399-a032-a13fee29e012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16f19f36-8376-5419-9c05-595c1dfe5d3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350322" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{18}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-6(0-\\frac{1}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2401ff7-53d7-5fc9-93dc-af89ac1a8b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288877} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c69c26-abf4-5aec-b4bf-24a816db0a50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8660f5f1-f7ea-5da0-ac60-28e25b93ef09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta +1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $\\theta \\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta +1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{(5\\theta +1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61687b0-9008-5ef3-a7d4-e1635208a08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288880} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4fe2876-2127-51de-8836-dd27c832933e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ffe81441-8aee-5b69-8444-b663eb3c8065" :question/math-academy-id "q-350534" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}+2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{({x}^{4}+2)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{1/2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1/2}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417ab5fa-7d06-5fbe-a44a-d34b28a1ba14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288883} {:answer/id #uuid "f595509e-0c53-5e8d-a745-b731185df427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417ab5fa-7d06-5fbe-a44a-d34b28a1ba14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288883} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6087e5e-901a-59e8-9916-5f25507a9f3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "823ca2d9-cebd-5456-8ab8-8f846682425f" :question/math-academy-id "q-350542" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=4t+3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4t+3$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{(4t+3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{5}{2{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-3}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78432dd-fefe-5420-a212-8cf893e9ddf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288887} {:answer/id #uuid "5404ad5f-58e9-533b-9474-d96ddb48ece6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78432dd-fefe-5420-a212-8cf893e9ddf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288887} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3e5c2b-3a58-5ee8-8d1d-9b386e6cb07c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d17f08ab-cd4a-5a3a-8655-e5472408f6cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350546" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-12$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3}-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-12}\\frac{3}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-12}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-12} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-12)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1e4e9b9-ff11-5c8c-9300-54559b516c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081783} {:answer/id #uuid "b19b98c6-86e8-582a-a00c-709f267cf8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081784} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb5fb2c-5346-5555-8f3a-2b08f51c8270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081785} {:answer/id #uuid "21224a12-463d-54a2-b768-c0405e3d228a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081786} {:answer/id #uuid "6d19dd48-9c08-5132-b4cd-1306b77ddccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d19dd48-9c08-5132-b4cd-1306b77ddccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081787} :answer-field/id #uuid "14cc958a-daab-50e9-8f16-d653bb22018d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e5736b-250b-5f2d-96b7-c89915cce3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-5962" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{\\theta }^{3}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}3{\\theta }^{-3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{3}{2}{\\theta }^{-2})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86525533-bd5b-5065-89f0-8788651ce48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081831} {:answer/id #uuid "996531bc-edb5-5093-b4b8-1db7bbe69f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081832} {:answer/id #uuid "21b23561-bed6-51fb-b64f-574e18912063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081833} {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca8ddf-a39b-592f-b35e-9899f82240eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536796e4-8bd5-5fea-9a7f-68e198cabaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081834} :answer-field/id #uuid "9038bffe-853f-51eb-a75b-c72d158db212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998df1b1-461f-5fc4-87ac-c188c53c68e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-6032" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{10}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{10}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{10-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-6) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}]}
